;; A WORKED EXAMPLE shipped with the seed — not a configuration. Copy the one
;; matching your seat to resources/profile.edn and edit it.
;;
;; SEAT :claude. Its sibling is agy-ide.edn, and the pair is instructive: they
;; are near mirror images. Here Anthropic writes and Google reviews; there
;; Google writes and Anthropic reviews. Between them both tool-call adapters
;; are exercised, which is why there are two rather than one.
;;
;; Model names are dated selections (decision log P0-3: "Specific models are
;; dated selections"). The SHAPE is the part meant to outlive them.
;;
;; Three things this file is careful about:
;;
;;   ONE SEAT. The interactive client is where the human sits. The three
;;   dispatched roles are reached over HTTP by the harness and are not anyone's
;;   editor — so there is exactly one :seat, and it is a scalar so it cannot
;;   grow a second. :seat names a tool `bb doctor` knows how to probe, which is
;;   why it is :claude rather than :claude-code — doctor probes binaries.
;;
;;   THE CODER'S FAMILY FOLLOWS THE SEAT, by convention and not by rule. You
;;   are already being helped by that family interactively, so letting it write
;;   the code too keeps one story; what must then differ is the VERIFIERS. It
;;   is not checked, because OpenCode 2 and Pi are bring-your-own-model clients
;;   with no inherent family — the relation is not expressible for half the
;;   seats this kit supports.
;;
;;   VERIFIER ≠ CODER FAMILY. Method §05 and decision log P0-3, and this one IS
;;   checked. The Tester and the Reviewer both verify the Coder, so neither may
;;   share its family. `bb profile` is the only place that rule has ever been
;;   anything but prose.
;;
;;   NO KEYS, EVER. :key-env NAMES an environment variable. This file is
;;   committed; a field that could hold a secret would eventually be handed one.

{:seat :claude

 :roles
 {;; Direct to Anthropic, which is the :anthropic tool-call shape.
  ;;
  ;; CLAUDE FABLE 5.1 AT MEDIUM EFFORT, replacing claude-sonnet-5 (2026-09-14).
  ;; Three things differ from the model it replaced, and each is set here or
  ;; handled in harness.adapter:
  ;;
  ;;   THINKING IS ALWAYS ON, and thinking tokens count against :max_tokens. The
  ;;   adapter's default of 4096 would cut a turn off mid-thought, so 16000 — the
  ;;   non-streaming ceiling the API guidance gives.
  ;;
  ;;   NO FALLBACK MODEL, on purpose. The API can re-route a refused request to
  ;;   another model; here that would make a Coder dispatch answered by a model
  ;;   this file does not name, and every report says which model answered. A
  ;;   refusal fails the dispatch instead, and the adapter says why.
  ;;
  ;;   COST IS COMPUTED, NOT REPORTED. Anthropic direct returns usage and no
  ;;   cost, so :pricing below carries the list prices, with their source and
  ;;   date, and the report shows usage × price marked with a ~. Re-read the
  ;;   page when the model or the date changes; nothing checks it for you.
  :coder {:family :anthropic
          :model "claude-fable-5-1"
          :shape :anthropic
          :endpoint "https://api.anthropic.com"
          :key-env "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"
          ;; EFFORT LOW, from 2026-09-14 (D17). Medium was the first setting
          ;; (D14–D16); low is the trade the user chose after a day on both.
          ;; D17 is D15 rerun with only this changed, to see whether a
          ;; low-effort Coder still flags a contract conflict.
          :params {:output_config {:effort "low"}
                   :max_tokens 16000}
          ;; USD per million tokens: base input, output, 5-minute and 1-hour
          ;; cache writes, cache reads (0.025x on Fable 5.1; 0.1x elsewhere).
          :pricing {:per-mtok {:in 10 :out 50
                               :cache-write-5m 12.5 :cache-write-1h 20
                               :cache-read 0.25}
                    :source "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing"
                    :as-of "2026-09-14"}}

  ;; CHOOSE THE TESTER BY ITS TESTS, NOT ITS PRICE, and run D4 is why this
  ;; says so. It had `deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash` here; that model hit its
  ;; iteration cap on all three attempts, broke two conventions its own
  ;; system prompt states, and failed twice on the same exception with the
  ;; gate output handed straight back. The Tester writes MORE than the Coder —
  ;; 195 lines against 28 in that run — from the contract alone, under more
  ;; lint conventions. The cheapest model was in the hardest seat.
  ;;
  ;; gpt-5.6-sol WAS CHOSEN THAT WAY, by bake-off B1 (RUNS.md): same packet
  ;; for every candidate, tests gated against a known-good implementation and
  ;; run against eight mutants. It and gpt-5.3-codex both killed all eight;
  ;; this one did it in 9 iterations for $0.035, against 21 and $0.175 — on
  ;; the flex endpoint, which this profile no longer uses. glm-5.3 capped
  ;; having written nothing. It replaced gpt-5.2-codex, which never
  ;; wrote a generative test in D7. One task, one sample each.
  ;;
  ;; STANDARD, NOT FLEX, AND THAT IS MEASURED. B1 ran on :only ["openai/flex"].
  ;; The first dispatches through that pin were rate-limited upstream: flex
  ;; refused 3 of 4 requests inside 75s and 4 of 5 dispatches in a row, while
  ;; this endpoint answered every time. Flex is not worth a Tester that
  ;; fails for capacity rather than for its tests.
  ;;
  ;; "openai" IS ALSO THE PROVIDER'S NAME, so this pin is observed rather than
  ;; guaranteed to exclude its flex and fast endpoints: seven requests through
  ;; it all reached the same standard endpoint at service tier "default". The
  ;; report shows any other tier beside the provider, so a routing change
  ;; would be visible. A pin at all because D4 drew two different hosts for
  ;; one slug across three attempts, and "the model failed" was never a safe
  ;; reading after that.
  :tester {:family :openai
           :model "openai/gpt-5.6-sol"
           :shape :openai
           :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
           :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
           :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                    :provider {:only ["openai"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}

  :reviewer {:family :google
             :model "google/gemini-3.8-flash"
             :shape :openai
             :endpoint "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1"
             :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
             ;; MEDIUM, NOT HIGH, AND THAT IS MEASURED. The original sketch put
             ;; :reasoning_effort "high" on every OpenRouter role, unexamined. On
             ;; gemini-3.8-flash as Reviewer it produced 27,706 tokens, 4m09s and an
             ;; EMPTY final message; the identical dispatch at medium answered in 63s
             ;; for half the money with real findings. High reasoning on a flash tier
             ;; spends the budget thinking and has nothing left to say with — D1 saw
             ;; the same shape when a small max_tokens returned empty content.
             :params {:reasoning_effort "medium"
                      :provider {:only ["google-vertex"] :allow_fallbacks false}}}}}
